<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French Revolution was the period of upheaval in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that began with the meeting of the Estates-General in 1789, abolished the monarchy in 1792 and ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s seizure of power in 1799. Its ideas of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, equality and the rights of man spread far beyond France, inspiring settlers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graaff-reinet">Graaff-Reinet</a> and Swellendam, while the wars it began reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and its colonies for a generation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-revolution.jpg" alt="French Revolution" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>French Revolution</b> the Republic refused in 1793 to accept the quèstia, rejecting the French co-sovereignty as a feudal relic, and for over a decade the bishop was the only co-prince until Napoleon restored the French rights in 1806 at the Andorrans' request.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Norman customary law was written down around 1200 in the Très ancien coutumier and later in the Grand coutumier de Normandie, which in revised form remained the law of the province until the <b>French Revolution</b> and still underlies law in the Channel Islands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Inspired by the <b>French Revolution</b>, the Society of United Irishmen led a rebellion in 1798, supported by a small French landing in County Mayo; it was suppressed with heavy loss of life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Inspired by the <b>French Revolution</b>, the Society of United Irishmen sought a republic uniting Catholics, Protestants and Dissenters, and Wolfe Tone obtained French support; a French fleet reached Bantry Bay in December 1796 but storms prevented a landing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferdinand IV, who succeeded in 1759 when his father Charles left to become king of Spain, and his wife Maria Carolina of Austria, sister of Marie Antoinette, turned against reformers after the <b>French Revolution</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>French Revolution</b> brought the crisis to a head.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
